mode: "100644"
text: "# Operating Rhythm\n\nA calendar is only as good as the workflow that keeps it running. The skeleton tells you *what* to\npost; the operating rhythm is *how the work actually happens* week to week — and it's what separates\na calendar that lasts from one that's abandoned by week three.\n\n## The planning ritual\n\nSet a recurring, time-boxed planning cadence so content never depends on inspiration:\n\n- **Monthly (≈60–90 min):** look at the month — anchors (launches, events), the theme/arc, any\n  series adjustments. Decide the shape.\n- **Weekly (≈30 min):** fill next week's slots from the skeleton into actual briefs (this is the\n  hand-off to `batch-content-plan`), and pick what goes in the reactive slots.\n- **Daily (≈10–15 min):** engage — reply to comments/DMs early (especially the first hour after\n  posting). Engagement is part of the rhythm, not an afterthought.\n\nPut these on the calendar as recurring blocks. The ritual *is* the system.\n\n## Batch creation\n\nCreate in focused batches rather than scrambling each day:\n\n- Group like work (film all TikToks in one session; write all carousels in another) to cut\n  context-switching.\n- Aim to produce a week (or more) at once, then schedule it out.\n- Batching is what makes a buffer possible.\n\n## The buffer / queue\n\nStay **ahead** of live:\n\n- Keep at least a few days — ideally a week+ — of content queued in WoopSocial.\n- The buffer absorbs bad weeks: when life happens, the queue keeps posting and the streak holds.\n- Refill the buffer during the weekly ritual before it runs dry.\n\nA buffer turns \"consistency\" from a willpower problem into a system property.\n\n## Review & iterate (honestly)\n\nImprove the calendar over time — without overclaiming data:\n\n- WoopSocial post analytics covers **TikTok, LinkedIn, Facebook, Instagram, Pinterest, YouTube, and Bluesky**, but those are lifetime post counts, not a\n  best-time model. Don't pretend the calendar is optimized to \"our best times.\" Review winners via\n  `analytics-and-reporting`, and use **native insights** for metrics WoopSocial doesn't store, or\n  **qualitative judgment** (what resonated, what drained you, what's sustainable).\n- Each month, ask: which slots/series are working? which drain effort for little return? Is the\n  cadence still sustainable? Adjust the skeleton — kill what isn't working, double down on what is.\n- Treat timing/cadence tweaks as **experiments to observe**, not optimizations you can prove.\n\n## Roles (if a team)\n\nIf more than one person is involved, assign the recurring functions: **create · edit · schedule ·\nengage · review.** Ambiguity here is where team calendars stall. One owner per slot type.\n\n## Hand-offs\n\nThe calendar is the top of a chain it doesn't execute alone:\n\n- **Fill a specific period →** `batch-content-plan` (turns slots into briefs).\n- **Write each piece →** the content skills (`caption-writer`, `reels-script`, `tiktok-script`,\n  `carousel-writer`, `thread-writer`, `linkedin-post-writer`).\n- **Adapt across platforms →** `cross-platform-repurposing`.\n- **Schedule/publish →** `scheduling-and-queue` (into the buffer/queue).\n- **Reactive slots →** `trend-jacking`.\n\nThe content-calendar skill owns the **recurring structure and rhythm**; these own execution.\n"
